\chapter{Accounts}\label{chap:accounts}

The Accounts concept owns what an account \emph{is} and the only operations
that move a balance. It knows nothing about transactions --- that ignorance is
deliberate (see the architecture chapter): the store of value must not depend
on the rules that govern movement, or the two could never be reasoned about
apart.

\section{The public seam}

Everything another concept may call lives in one header. An account is opened
by name and referred to afterwards by a small integer id. Balances are signed
\texttt{long} cents. Four operations are exposed: open an account, read a
balance, apply a signed delta, and --- so a caller can validate before it
mutates --- test whether an id refers to a live account.

@d Accounts: header @{#ifndef LEDGER_ACCOUNTS_H
#define LEDGER_ACCOUNTS_H

#define ACC_MAX 64              /* the store holds at most this many accounts */

int         acc_open(const char *name); /* -> id >= 0, or -1 if the store is full */
long        acc_balance(int id);        /* balance in cents, 0 for an unknown id */
int         acc_apply(int id, long delta); /* add delta cents; 0 ok, -1 bad id  */
int         acc_exists(int id);         /* 1 if id is a live account, else 0     */
const char *acc_name(int id);           /* the account's name, or "?"            */

#endif
@}

\section{The store}

The implementation is a fixed array of accounts. A real ledger would persist
to a journal (Stage 3); the walking skeleton keeps the store in memory, which
is enough to prove the invariant. Note that \texttt{acc\_apply} is neutral: it
adds a delta and asks no questions about balance. The rule that deltas must
come in balanced sets belongs to Ledger, not here.

@d Accounts: implementation @{#include "accounts.h"
#include <string.h>

static struct { char name[32]; long balance; int used; } accounts[ACC_MAX];

int acc_open(const char *name) {
    for (int i = 0; i < ACC_MAX; i++) {
        if (!accounts[i].used) {
            accounts[i].used = 1;
            accounts[i].balance = 0;
            strncpy(accounts[i].name, name, sizeof accounts[i].name - 1);
            accounts[i].name[sizeof accounts[i].name - 1] = '\0';
            return i;
        }
    }
    return -1;
}

int acc_exists(int id) {
    return id >= 0 && id < ACC_MAX && accounts[id].used;
}

long acc_balance(int id) {
    return acc_exists(id) ? accounts[id].balance : 0;
}

int acc_apply(int id, long delta) {
    if (!acc_exists(id)) return -1;
    accounts[id].balance += delta;
    return 0;
}

const char *acc_name(int id) {
    return acc_exists(id) ? accounts[id].name : "?";
}
@}

\section{Seed: the demo accounts}

The walking skeleton needs two accounts to move money between. Opening them is
an Accounts concern, so the snippet lives here and Api composes it. When this
chunk is expanded inside \texttt{main}, it declares \texttt{cash} and
\texttt{revenue} in that scope.

@d Accounts: demo accounts @{int cash    = acc_open("cash");
    int revenue = acc_open("revenue");@}
